The linker error `undefined reference to '__device_dts_ord_30'` indicates that a device driver is trying to reference a device from the devicetree, but the device structure for that node was never created. This typically happens when a devicetree node with `status = "okay"` is missing a `compatible` property, so the build system doesn't know which driver to associate with it and consequently doesn't instantiate a `struct device`.

In this case, the `espi_emul` controller driver iterates over its child nodes in the devicetree and uses `DEVICE_DT_GET()` to get a pointer to each child's device structure. The child node `espi-host@0` in `tests/drivers/espi/boards/native_sim.overlay` is missing its `compatible` property. The test provides a stub driver in `tests/drivers/espi/src/stub_espi_emul_host.c` which is compatible with `"zephyr,espi-emul-espi-host"`.

The fix is to add the correct `compatible` property to the `espi-host@0` node in the overlay file.

tests/drivers/espi/boards/native_sim.overlay
<<<<<<<< SEARCH
&espi0 {
	espi_host: espi-host@0 {
		status = "okay";
		reg = <0x0>;
	};
};
========
&espi0 {
	espi_host: espi-host@0 {
		compatible = "zephyr,espi-emul-espi-host";
		status = "okay";
		reg = <0x0>;
	};
};
